lands. This tumor is characterized by the excessive production of catecholamines, which can lead to various clinical symptoms, including hypertension and hypertensive crises. It is often diagnosed through biochemical profiling and is important to rule out before surgical procedures, such as those for medullary thyroid carcinoma.\n\nPatients with FEOCROMOCITOMA may experience a range of symptoms due to the overproduction of catecholamines, and the condition is associated with certain genetic syndromes, including Cowden syndrome. Screening for this tumor is recommended starting at the age of 11 for individuals at risk. Treatment typically involves surgical intervention, which is necessary after proper medical preparation to manage the symptoms and potential complications, such as hypercalcemia due to excess PTHrP secretion.\n\nOverall, FEOCROMOCITOMA requires specific diagnostic and therapeutic approaches due to its potential malignancy and the serious health implications associated with its symptoms.\",46\r\n5161,METANEFRINAS,\"METANEFRINAS are biochemical markers utilized in the diagnosis of adrenal tumors and related conditions, particularly pheochromocytoma and paragangliomas. They are metabolites of catecholamines, which are substances produced by the adrenal glands. These metabolites are typically measured in urine tests, serving as a diagnostic marker for pheochromocytoma, a type of tumor that can affect adrenal function. Additionally, metanephrines are assessed in laboratory tests to evaluate patients with adrenal incidentalomas, helping to identify potential underlying tumors. Overall, the measurement of metanephrines plays a crucial role in the diagnostic process for various adrenal-related conditions.\",4\r\n5170,CRESPO HERNÁNDEZ I,\"Crespo Hernández I is an author recognized for contributions to the medical literature concerning pheochromocytomas and paragangliomas. This individual has been referenced in various contexts related to these conditions, particularly noted for their work in the \"\"Manual de Endocrinología y Nutrición.\"\" Through these contributions, Crespo Hernández I has played a significant role in advancing the understanding of these endocrine tumors.\",2\r\n5169,DÍAZ PÉREZ JA,\"Díaz Pérez JA is an author recognized for his contributions to the medical literature on pheochromocytomas and paragangliomas. He has specifically written about these topics in the \"\"Manual de Endocrinología y Nutrición,\"\" highlighting his expertise in the field. His work is frequently referenced in discussions surrounding these endocrine tumors, underscoring his significance in advancing knowledge related to these conditions.\",2\r\n5241,GEN VHL,\"GEN VHL refers to the VHL gene, which is a crucial tumor suppressor gene. When mutated, it is associated with various neoplasms, including feocromocitoma. The VHL gene is also linked to von Hippel-Lindau syndrome, a genetic condition that can lead to the development of multiple tumors. This gene plays a significant role in the genetic assessment of patients with adrenal tumors, highlighting its importance in both cancer predisposition and clinical evaluation.\",2\r\n5252,ANATOMOPATOLÓGICO,\"Anatomopathological report refers to the examination of tissues to diagnose diseases, including tumors like feocromocitomas.\",1\r\n5171,CALCIOANTAGONISTAS,\"Calcioantagonistas are medications that can influence catecholamine levels, which may result in false positives in diagnostic tests for pheochromocytomas. Additionally, these medications can affect vascular resistance and may interfere with the diagnosis of pheochromocytoma, particularly when administered prior to MIBG testing.\",1\r\n5175,CRISIS HIPERTENSIVAS,Crisis hipertensivas are episodes of high blood pressure that can occur in patients with feocromocitomas and require careful management.,1\r\n5163,FENOXIBENZAMIDA,\"Fenoxibenzamida is a medication specifically utilized for the preoperative management of pheochromocytoma, a type of